# The worked example of UGM's `docs/rules-design.md` §8 and §13, in the surface.
#
# What it is here to show is the BOUNDARY: the agent does not believe its user.
# What arrives is that a channel said something; turning that into a claim about
# the world is `<trust-user>`, an ordinary rule -- so it can be argued with,
# overridden, or simply not written.
#
#   /load corpus/weather.ugm
#   /run
#   say user: +raining(here)
#   /run
#   /why raining(here)
#   /graph talk
#
# The last two are the interesting ones. `raining(here)` is held, and the trail
# says on whose word -- not "because it is true" but "because user said so, and
# because a rule says to believe the user".

# §8 <R2>. `implies`, so the conclusion lands in the SAME moment and is derived
# rather than brought about. Written as `causes`, the backward reader would
# produce a plan to make it rain by making it cloudy, and the two-connective
# split is exactly what makes that plan unwritable.
rule <weather> = implies(
    { +cloudy(?day, morning) },
    { +likely(rain(?day, afternoon)) } )

# §13. The consequent is a bare variable: whatever the channel says, believe it.
# ⭐ This is the rule to delete first if you want to see what the split buys.
# Without it the arrival is still on the record -- `/graph talk` shows
# `arrived(user, ...)` and `says(user, ...)` -- and nothing about the world
# follows from it. The agent heard and did not believe, which is a state most
# systems cannot represent at all.
rule <trust-user> = implies(
    { +says(user, ?p, plus) },
    { +?p } )

# Standing knowledge, stamped as having come from the knowledge base.
fact +cloudy(monday, morning)

# An arrival, authored. Typing `say user: +raining(here)` at the prompt does
# exactly this, which is the point of the input language being the corpus
# language: an interactive session is one document that arrives slowly.
say user: +raining(here)
